{
  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "### Book Summary\n**Title:** The Deafening Silence: Non-Verbal Communication in Four Pacific Societies  \n**Author:** Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri  \n**Publication Year:** 2019  \n**Publisher:** Oceanic University Press  \n**ISBN:** 978-1-83748-291-5  \n\n**Overview:**  \nThis scholarly monograph argues that silence represents not just the absence of speech but one of communication's most structurally complex forms. Dr. Tāwhiri draws upon twelve years of ethnographic fieldwork conducted across Polynesian, Micronesian, and Melanesian communities to present a six-category typology of communicative silence. This includes concepts such as **nooanoa** (held relational silence) and **faka'apa'apa** (respectful deferential silence). The book delves into how each form of silence is interwoven with kinship obligations, ritual practices, and everyday social interactions. An extended vignette from Fakarava atoll in the opening chapter establishes the central methodological claim that silence must be studied autonomously, rather than as a mere residual element in linguistic analysis.\n\n**Key Themes:**\n- Non-verbal communication\n- Communicative silence\n- Pacific anthropology\n- Ethnolinguistics\n- Kinship and ritual\n- Indigenous epistemology\n- Typology of silence\n\n### Author Background\n**Dr. Mereana Tāwhiri** (Te Reo: Mereana Tāwhiri-Paea) is an Associate Professor of Linguistic Anthropology at the University of Auckland and an affiliate researcher at the Australian National University. She specializes in non-verbal and silence-based communication systems in Oceanic cultures. Dr. Tāwhiri obtained her PhD from ANU in 2006 under Professor David Holton and has previously published numerous peer-reviewed articles in reputable journals, including *Language in Society*, *American Ethnologist*, and *Pacific Studies*.\n\n**For More Information:**\n- [University of Auckland Faculty Profile](https://openbooks.net/authors/mereana-tawhiri)\n- [Pacific Anthropology Network Profile](https://openbooks.net/scholars/mereana-tawhiri)\n\nThis information should provide a solid foundation for your seminar on linguistic anthropology.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
